{"task_id": "Mbpp/235", "solution": "def even_bit_set_number(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Set all even-positioned bits (0, 2, 4, 6, etc.) to 1.\n    This operation uses a bitwise OR with a mask of all even bits set.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a mask with 1s at all even bit positions (0, 2, 4, ..., 62).\n    # This corresponds to 0x5555555555555555 in hexadecimal (a 64-bit mask).\n    # Python's arbitrary precision integers handle numbers larger than this mask,\n    # effectively extending the mask with leading zeros for the OR operation.\n    even_mask = 0x5555555555555555\n    \n    # Perform a bitwise OR operation to set all even bits of the number to 1.\n    return number | even_mask"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/235", "solution": "def even_bit_set_number(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets all even-positioned bits (0, 2, 4, etc.) of a given number to 1.\n    This is achieved by performing a bitwise OR operation with a mask\n    that has 1s at all even bit positions.\n    \"\"\"\n    # A mask with 1s at all even positions (0, 2, 4, ...).\n    # In binary, 0x5555... is 01010101..., which sets bits at positions 0, 2, 4, etc.\n    # Python integers have arbitrary precision, so a sufficiently large mask\n    # (e.g., a 64-bit mask) is used to cover common integer sizes.\n    even_bits_mask = 0x5555555555555555\n    return number | even_bits_mask"}
