{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the given number.\n    It calculates the square root, finds the next integer, and then squares it.\n    \"\"\"\n    root = math.sqrt(number)\n    # The next_integer must be strictly greater than root if number is a perfect square,\n    # or the ceiling of root if number is not a perfect square.\n    # math.floor(root) + 1 handles both cases correctly.\n    next_integer_root = math.floor(root) + 1\n    return int(next_integer_root ** 2)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number: float) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the next perfect square greater than the given number.\n    This is achieved by calculating the square root, taking its floor, adding one, and then squaring the result.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Calculate the square root of the input number.\n    sqrt_val = math.sqrt(number)\n    \n    # Determine the smallest integer whose square will be strictly greater than 'number'.\n    # This is effectively ceil(sqrt_val) if 'number' is not a perfect square,\n    # or (sqrt_val + 1) if 'number' is a perfect square.\n    # Both cases are covered by floor(sqrt_val) + 1.\n    next_root = math.floor(sqrt_val) + 1\n    \n    # Square this integer to get the next perfect square.\n    return int(next_root * next_root)"}
