{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "def newman_prime(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth Newman-Shanks-Williams prime number based on the problem's specific indexing.\n    The 'n' maps to the (n-2)-th prime in the 0-indexed list of NSW primes.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime.\"\"\"\n        if number < 2:\n            return False\n        if number == 2:\n            return True\n        if number % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        i = 3\n        while i * i <= number:\n            if number % i == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 2\n        return True\n\n    def generate_nsw_sequence():\n        \"\"\"Generates Newman-Shanks-Williams sequence values using a generator.\"\"\"\n        s0, s1 = 1, 1\n        yield s0\n        yield s1\n        while True:\n            sn = 2 * s1 + s0\n            yield sn\n            s0 = s1\n            s1 = sn\n\n    # Apply the specific mapping required by the assertion newman_prime(3) == 7.\n    # This implies that n=3 requests the prime at index 1 (0-indexed) from the list of NSW primes [3, 7, 17, ...].\n    # So, the target_prime_idx is (n - 2).\n    target_prime_idx = n - 2\n    \n    # As per instructions, no explicit checks or error handling for edge cases (like n=1,\n    # which would result in target_prime_idx = -1 and an infinite loop) are added.\n\n    nsw_gen = generate_nsw_sequence()\n    \n    primes_found_count = 0\n    \n    # Consume the first two NSW terms (S0=1, S1=1), as they are not prime and are initial terms.\n    # This effectively starts prime checking from S2 = 3.\n    next(nsw_gen) # Consume S0 = 1\n    next(nsw_gen) # Consume S1 = 1\n\n    while True:\n        current_nsw = next(nsw_gen)\n        if is_prime(current_nsw):\n            if primes_found_count == target_prime_idx:\n                return current_nsw\n            primes_found_count += 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/264", "solution": "def dog_age(human_age):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates a dog's age in equivalent dog years using a common conversion formula.\n    The first two human years are calculated at 10.5 dog years per year, and subsequent years at 4 dog years per year.\n    \"\"\"\n    if human_age <= 2:\n        return human_age * 10.5\n    else:\n        # For ages greater than 2, it's 21 dog years for the first two years\n        # plus 4 dog years for each year beyond the second.\n        return 21 + (human_age - 2) * 4"}
