{"task_id": "Mbpp/283", "solution": "def validate(integer: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the frequency of each digit in the integer is less than or equal to the digit itself.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def extract_digits(num: int) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Converts an integer to its string representation.\"\"\"\n        return str(num)\n\n    def count_frequencies(digit_str: str) -> dict[int, int]:\n        \"\"\"Counts the frequency of each digit in a string.\"\"\"\n        frequencies = {}\n        for char_digit in digit_str:\n            digit = int(char_digit)\n            frequencies[digit] = frequencies.get(digit, 0) + 1\n        return frequencies\n\n    def is_frequency_valid(digit_value: int, frequency_count: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a digit's frequency is less than or equal to its value.\"\"\"\n        return frequency_count <= digit_value\n\n    digits_string = extract_digits(integer)\n    digit_frequencies = count_frequencies(digits_string)\n\n    for digit_value, frequency_count in digit_frequencies.items():\n        if not is_frequency_valid(digit_value, frequency_count):\n            return False\n            \n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/283", "solution": "def validate(integer: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates if frequency of each digit is <= digit value itself.\n    Returns: True if all digit frequencies are valid, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    def count_digit_frequencies(integer: int) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"\n        Count frequency of each digit in the integer.\n        Returns: dict mapping digit to frequency count.\n        \"\"\"\n        frequencies = {i: 0 for i in range(10)}\n        if integer == 0: # Special case for 0\n            frequencies[0] = 1\n            return frequencies\n\n        s_integer = str(abs(integer)) # Handle negative numbers by using abs\n        for char_digit in s_integer:\n            digit = int(char_digit)\n            frequencies[digit] += 1\n        return frequencies\n\n    def is_frequency_valid(frequencies: dict) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Check if all digit frequencies are <= their digit values.\n        Returns: True if all frequencies valid, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        for digit, count in frequencies.items():\n            if digit == 0 and count > 0 and integer != 0: # 0 has no special restriction unless it's the only digit\n                # If 0 is present but not the only digit or the integer itself, it's checked like any other digit\n                # The prompt implies 0 count must be <= 0, which is only possible if 0 is not present.\n                # However, a common interpretation is that a digit like '0' should have its frequency\n                # compared to the digit value '0'. If it appears, its frequency is > 0, so it would fail\n                # unless explicitly handled. The example `1234` implies no 0s.\n                # Let's assume the rule applies strictly: if digit 0 appears, its frequency must be <= 0.\n                # This means if 0 appears at all, it's invalid unless 0 has no frequency (count=0).\n                # But that seems contradictory to having a 0 digit.\n                # A more common interpretation: for digits > 0. If 0 is present (count > 0), it invalidates IF 0 count > 0.\n                # The assert validate(1234) == True implies we don't worry about 0 being in the number unless specified.\n                # If we take the prompt literally \"frequency of each digit ... is less than or equal to the digit itself\",\n                # then if digit '0' appears, its frequency will be > 0. Since 0 > 0 is False, it should return False.\n                # This would mean any number containing '0' like 101 would be False.\n                # Let's stick to the strict interpretation based on \"each digit\".\n                # If a digit '0' is present (count > 0), then `count <= digit` (e.g., `1 <= 0`) is False.\n                # So any number with a 0 digit would return False.\n                # For example, validate(10) -> count_digit_frequencies(10) gives {0:1, 1:1}.\n                # For digit=0, count=1. `1 <= 0` is False. So return False.\n                # This seems like the most consistent interpretation.\n                pass # Default behavior for 0 digit will handle it\n\n            if count > digit:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    digit_frequencies = count_digit_frequencies(integer)\n    return is_frequency_valid(digit_frequencies)"}
