{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "def max_sum_increasing_subseq(array, length, i, k):\n    # dp[x] will store the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at index x,\n    # considering elements from array[0] to array[x].\n    dp = [0] * (i + 1)\n\n    # Initialize dp array: each element itself forms an increasing subsequence of sum array[x].\n    for x in range(i + 1):\n        dp[x] = array[x]\n\n    # Fill the dp array for the prefix array[0...i].\n    # For each element array[x], iterate through previous elements array[prev_x].\n    # If array[x] can extend an increasing subsequence ending at array[prev_x],\n    # update dp[x] if a larger sum is found.\n    for x in range(i + 1):\n        for prev_x in range(x):\n            if array[x] > array[prev_x]:\n                dp[x] = max(dp[x], dp[prev_x] + array[x])\n\n    # Find the maximum sum from dp[0...i] such that the last element (array[x])\n    # is strictly less than array[k]. This allows array[k] to extend the subsequence.\n    max_prev_sum = 0\n    for x in range(i + 1):\n        if array[k] > array[x]:\n            max_prev_sum = max(max_prev_sum, dp[x])\n\n    # The final result is the maximum sum found from the prefix,\n    # plus the mandatory element array[k]. If no valid prefix subsequence was found\n    # (i.e., no array[x] < array[k]), then max_prev_sum remains 0,\n    # and the result is just array[k] itself.\n    return max_prev_sum + array[k]"}
