{"task_id": "Mbpp/739", "solution": "def find_Index(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the index of the smallest triangular number with exactly n digits.\n    It iterates through triangular numbers, checking their digit count.\n    \"\"\"\n    def calculate_triangular_number(k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the k-th triangular number.\"\"\"\n        return k * (k + 1) // 2\n\n    def count_digits(number: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Counts the number of digits in an integer.\"\"\"\n        return len(str(number))\n\n    k = 1\n    while True:\n        triangular_num = calculate_triangular_number(k)\n        digit_count = count_digits(triangular_num)\n\n        if digit_count == n:\n            return k\n        elif digit_count > n:\n            # If the current triangular number has more digits than n,\n            # it means we've overshot. This implies there's no triangular\n            # number with *exactly* n digits. However, the problem implies\n            # such a number always exists for valid n (e.g., n=2 returns 4).\n            # So, this condition should not be reached for typical n.\n            # We continue incrementing k until we find the first one.\n            # In cases where n could be very small and a T_k skips n digits,\n            # this check might be relevant, but for positive integers n,\n            # the digit count generally increases gradually.\n            pass # Keep searching for the first one that matches\n        k += 1"}
