{"task_id": "Mbpp/743", "solution": "def rotate_right(input_list, positions):\n    \"\"\"\n    Rotates a list to the right by specified number of positions.\n    Returns a new list with elements rotated right, leaving the original list unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    list_length = len(input_list)\n    if list_length == 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Calculate the effective number of positions to rotate using modulo to handle\n    # rotations greater than the list length.\n    effective_positions = positions % list_length\n\n    # Slice the list into two parts and concatenate them:\n    # 1. The elements that move from the end to the beginning.\n    # 2. The remaining elements that stay in their relative order.\n    return input_list[-effective_positions:] + input_list[:-effective_positions]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/743", "solution": "def rotate_right(lst: list, positions: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rotates a list to the right by a specified number of positions.\n    Returns a new list with the elements rotated.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(lst)\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Calculate the effective number of positions to rotate\n    # This handles cases where positions might be larger than the list length,\n    # or if positions is 0.\n    effective_positions = positions % n\n\n    # Use list slicing to perform the rotation\n    # The last 'effective_positions' elements move to the front,\n    # and the remaining elements follow.\n    return lst[-effective_positions:] + lst[:-effective_positions]"}
